     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Scheduling around the family's visit and evening rounds isn't a clinical reason for 50 mg. That dose is five times the maximum and could seriously harm the patient. Amlodipine also has a long half-life of 30 to 50 hours, so one dose can't be quickly reversed. An overdose can cause profound hypotension and shock that last for days.\n\nIf the goal is to get the patient's blood pressure under control before evening rounds, there are safer options:\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg** once daily is the maximum dose. Its full effect takes about 1 to 2 weeks, so a higher dose won't speed that up.\n- **A faster-acting agent** may fit better if the blood pressure needs urgent control. Examples are IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, or oral nifedipine or captopril, depending on the clinical picture. Pharmacy or the attending can help choose.\n- **A call to pharmacy or the attending** would help if you believe this patient needs an unusual regimen. They can review it and document the rationale.\n\nI can enter amlodipine at 5 mg or 10 mg once daily now, or another order you choose. Which would you like?"
      }
     ],
